// RULE: one concept, one spelling. An idempotency window is declared on the relation's
// `idem` column, in epochs (NL0215 requires it, NL0217 refuses a wall-clock unit). The
// transaction form takes the identity and nothing else.
// WHY IT MATTERS: `txn idem("k", window: 30.days)` parsed, bound its key, and its window then
// reached nothing — the interpreter counted it and ignored it, lowering dropped it, and
// NL0216's own suggestion text recommended this spelling in the very unit the column form
// refuses. A transaction written with a window had none, and two spellings of one concept
// with different rigor is a defect in the calculus rather than a style question.
// EXPECT: NL0218
// STAGE: check
fn sweep(a: Id<Account>, b: Id<Account>, m: Money<usd>) -> Result<TxnId, TxnError>
    ! { append, debit<usd>, credit<usd> }
{
    txn idem("sweep", window: 30.days) { post(debit(a, m)?, credit(b, m)) }
}
